WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.100
-You look gorgeous.
-So do you.

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:04.167
Oh, my God.
-Come on.

00:00:04.167 --> 00:00:05.467
I'm so happy when you're here.

00:00:05.467 --> 00:00:07.634
I want to talk to you
about "Saturday Night Live."

00:00:07.634 --> 00:00:09.000
-Oh, my God.
-You're hosting.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.100
This is the second time.
I'm looking forward to this.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.567
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:12.567 --> 00:00:14.300
This is the episode
I'm looking forward to.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:15.834
It's going to be my favorite
of the whole season.

00:00:15.834 --> 00:00:18.033
-Oh, my gosh, that's so sweet.
You know what's so funny?

00:00:18.033 --> 00:00:20.901
I mean, honestly,
I forget how hard it is.

00:00:20.901 --> 00:00:23.634
And I mean, you know,
you do your show every night,

00:00:23.634 --> 00:00:25.868
but because I haven't done
variety in a long time,

00:00:25.868 --> 00:00:27.334
I forget how fast-paced.

00:00:27.334 --> 00:00:30.067
And so I'm definitely
very nervous.

00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:32.000
[ Laughter ]

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.167
-But you could crush.
I've seen you do every --

00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:35.767
Do you remember
all the sketches?

00:00:35.767 --> 00:00:37.200
-You want to know
the truth is?

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:38.567
I seriously have a fear.

00:00:38.567 --> 00:00:41.501
Like, I can perform
for strangers better

00:00:41.501 --> 00:00:42.767
than if people I know come.

00:00:42.767 --> 00:00:45.434
I have a fear
of disappointing loved ones.

00:00:45.434 --> 00:00:46.968
-I think I have that too.
-You do?

00:00:46.968 --> 00:00:48.467
-Yeah.
-That's so interesting.

00:00:48.467 --> 00:00:50.234
-I don't like to know
if anyone's in the audience.

00:00:50.234 --> 00:00:51.501
-That's so funny.

00:00:51.501 --> 00:00:52.767
-If my parents would come visit,

00:00:52.767 --> 00:00:55.267
I go, "No, don't tell me till
after the show."

00:00:55.267 --> 00:00:56.701
-That's exactly how I am.

00:00:56.701 --> 00:00:58.133
-So are people
coming to the show?

00:00:58.133 --> 00:00:59.501
-Yes. Yes.

00:00:59.501 --> 00:01:01.834
And I'm like, oh! Every time
I hear more and more,

00:01:01.834 --> 00:01:02.901
I'm like, "I'm so scared."

00:01:02.901 --> 00:01:04.501
But my husband and kids
are coming, too.

00:01:04.501 --> 00:01:05.901
So I'm so excited about that.
-Fritz!

00:01:05.901 --> 00:01:08.734
-Yes, Fritz and Stella, Nolan.
-How old are the kids now?

00:01:08.734 --> 00:01:12.701
-My kids are -- Nolan just
turned 18 and Stella is 19!

00:01:12.701 --> 00:01:15.801
-Buddy, I can't even believe.
I have a picture of them here.

00:01:15.801 --> 00:01:18.868
-Yeah. Aww!
-What a great, beautiful family.

00:01:18.868 --> 00:01:21.000
Here you are here.
-Oh, that's so sweet.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.467
[ Audience awws ]
-Yeah.

00:01:22.467 --> 00:01:24.434
-Thank you.
-There's Fritz there too.

00:01:24.434 --> 00:01:26.067
Have you done
the host dinner yet?

00:01:26.067 --> 00:01:27.434
-Yes, we did the host dinner.

00:01:27.434 --> 00:01:29.100
Well, the host dinner
is where Lorne Michaels

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.033
takes the host of the show
out for dinner

00:01:31.033 --> 00:01:33.300
with a lot of the cast
Tuesday night.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:36.033
It's like a fancy restaurant.
And this is so funny.

00:01:36.033 --> 00:01:38.067
But I have a friend who,
when I was on the show,

00:01:38.067 --> 00:01:40.467
my friend Carrie Aizley used
to always

00:01:40.467 --> 00:01:43.133
ask me, like, about the host
and what they would eat.

00:01:43.133 --> 00:01:45.033
Like for example, one week

00:01:45.033 --> 00:01:46.467
it was Neve Campbell
and she was like,

00:01:46.467 --> 00:01:48.534
"What did she eat? What did she
eat at dinner, Molly?"

00:01:48.534 --> 00:01:50.000
And I would give
a full report.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:51.234
I was like, "Well,

00:01:51.234 --> 00:01:55.267
Neve Campbell ate a tomato
basil pasta,"

00:01:55.267 --> 00:01:56.667
and she's like, "Did she have
any wine?"

00:01:56.667 --> 00:01:59.000
I was like, "Yeah, she had
a glass of sauvi B."

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.667
And then, you know,
"What else? Did she eat bread?"

00:02:01.667 --> 00:02:03.334
So I do the whole thing.
-You would give the whole scoop.

00:02:03.334 --> 00:02:04.567
-So I have to
give her the rundown.

00:02:04.567 --> 00:02:07.767
Like, Chloe Fineman
does eat gluten.

00:02:07.767 --> 00:02:10.234
-Whoa!
-She ordered a Coca-Cola
at dinner.

00:02:10.234 --> 00:02:12.067
So, like, my friend
likes to know.

00:02:12.067 --> 00:02:13.067
She likes to know.
She'd be like,

00:02:13.067 --> 00:02:14.367
"What did Jimmy Fallon eat?"

00:02:14.367 --> 00:02:15.968
-I don't remember
my host dinners so much.

00:02:15.968 --> 00:02:18.400
-You don't?
-I remember -- No.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.367
Sometimes I wasn't
invited to them.

00:02:20.367 --> 00:02:21.767
-Oh, right.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:21.767 --> 00:02:23.734
Well, do you want to know
what I had for my host dinner?

00:02:23.734 --> 00:02:25.467
-Yes.
-Fried calamari.

00:02:25.467 --> 00:02:27.234
-Oh!
-A margarita.

00:02:27.234 --> 00:02:30.834
-Wow!
-And creamy pasta with garlic.

00:02:30.834 --> 00:02:32.234
Good night.
-Wow!

00:02:32.234 --> 00:02:33.467
That's what I'm talking about!

00:02:33.467 --> 00:02:34.901
-Doesn't that sound good?

00:02:35.868 --> 00:02:37.167
Isn't that good to know?

00:02:37.167 --> 00:02:39.834
-That's good to know.
-I like to know what people eat.

00:02:39.834 --> 00:02:41.334
-I was thinking about
all the characters

00:02:41.334 --> 00:02:43.033
and all the fun stuff we used
to do on "Saturday Night Live."

00:02:43.033 --> 00:02:44.968
And you used to make me
laugh more than anyone.

00:02:44.968 --> 00:02:47.801
But do you remember a character
named Jeannie Darcy?

00:02:47.801 --> 00:02:49.501
-Yes.
-She was a stand-up comedian.

00:02:49.501 --> 00:02:51.801
-Yes.
She's a bad stand-up comedian.

00:02:51.801 --> 00:02:53.801
-She was a bad stand-up
comedian,

00:02:53.801 --> 00:02:55.334
but one of my favorites.

00:02:55.334 --> 00:02:58.133
She would go and it's very hard
to write this and perform this.

00:02:58.133 --> 00:02:59.400
And can I tell you why?

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.901
Because you're
writing bad comedy.

00:03:01.901 --> 00:03:04.701
You're writing someone
struggling to do comedy.

00:03:04.701 --> 00:03:07.033
And it's only funny if it
doesn't get laughs and stuff.

00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:11.767
-Yes. And I actually did it
toward the end of my "SNL" run

00:03:11.767 --> 00:03:13.701
because I was like,
"Oh, it's so hard to be thinking

00:03:13.701 --> 00:03:16.000
about making stuff funny
all the time" that I thought,

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.801
"I want to do something
that's really dull,

00:03:17.801 --> 00:03:21.801
that gets no laughs at all.
Just a dull, bad comedian

00:03:21.801 --> 00:03:24.501
who really should not be
trying to pursue comedy.

00:03:24.501 --> 00:03:25.934
But she has such big dreams."

00:03:25.934 --> 00:03:28.801
But basically when I first
did it at the at the table,

00:03:28.801 --> 00:03:31.400
it did --
it was like -- it went so-so.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:34.400
But you and Will
were laughing so hard.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.601
So then I would really
just perform to make you

00:03:37.601 --> 00:03:38.834
and Will Ferrell laugh.

00:03:38.834 --> 00:03:41.000
It was just for you two.
Do you remember that?

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:42.834
Yeah, because the audience
didn't get it at first.

00:03:42.834 --> 00:03:44.501
They were like,
"What is this?"

00:03:44.501 --> 00:03:47.033
They were like,
"This isn't funny."

00:03:47.033 --> 00:03:48.267
And then you would come out

00:03:48.267 --> 00:03:51.367
and it was just for Jimmy Fallon
and Will Ferrell.

00:03:51.367 --> 00:03:53.100
-I'm crying thinking
about how funny it was.

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:54.501
It was the best thing.
-Oh, that's so sweet.

00:03:54.501 --> 00:03:57.434
-You used to make me laugh
so hard.

00:03:57.434 --> 00:03:58.734
-That's so sweet.

00:03:58.734 --> 00:04:00.601
-And you've done -- I mean,
obviously

00:04:00.601 --> 00:04:02.267
Mary Katherine Gallagher.

00:04:02.267 --> 00:04:06.601
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:06.601 --> 00:04:08.167
"I'm 50!"
-Yes.

00:04:08.167 --> 00:04:09.968
-I mean, "I'm 50."
I mean, stop it.

00:04:09.968 --> 00:04:12.067
I mean, everyone's --
Sally O'Malley.

00:04:12.067 --> 00:04:13.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:13.534 --> 00:04:15.000
It's just so many things.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.801
And I was thinking, like,
impressions that you've done.

00:04:17.801 --> 00:04:20.334
I remember you doing,
like, Courtney Love.

00:04:20.334 --> 00:04:22.234
-Oh, yeah, Courtney Love.
I did Courtney Love.

00:04:22.234 --> 00:04:24.133
And people would
always ask like,

00:04:24.133 --> 00:04:26.200
"Oh, if you do an impression
of somebody, do they get mad?"

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:27.534
And I was like,
"Yes, sometimes."

00:04:27.534 --> 00:04:30.100
Like, I did an impression
of Courtney Love on the show,

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:33.501
and then the real Courtney Love
showed up live and she was mad.

00:04:33.501 --> 00:04:34.968
She was like,
"Where's Molly?"

00:04:34.968 --> 00:04:37.601
She was going around
the studio, hunting me down.

00:04:37.601 --> 00:04:39.968
-No!
-Ready to maybe punch me
or something.

00:04:39.968 --> 00:04:41.334
And I was like, "I'm scared."
-Oh, stop it.

00:04:41.334 --> 00:04:42.968
-No, maybe not.

00:04:42.968 --> 00:04:45.667
But she was definitely looking.
No, maybe not punching.

00:04:45.667 --> 00:04:47.734
-Whoa!
-But definitely looking for me.

00:04:47.734 --> 00:04:49.100
-She's like,
"Where's Molly Shannon?"

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:51.667
-Yeah, yeah. And then I saw her
and I'm dressed as her

00:04:51.667 --> 00:04:53.300
and I was like, "Hi."

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:57.767
And, you know, and she smelled
like witchy oils

00:04:57.767 --> 00:04:59.000
and she was tall.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.334
She was, like, tough with a --
And I was like,

00:05:01.334 --> 00:05:03.133
"Give me a cigarette."
And she gave me a cigarette.

00:05:03.133 --> 00:05:05.534
And then we smoked together
and I told her

00:05:05.534 --> 00:05:07.734
why it was such a compliment
that it's, you know,

00:05:07.734 --> 00:05:09.167
when somebody does
an impression of you,

00:05:09.167 --> 00:05:11.133
that's like cool, you know?
And she was like, "Yeah, yeah.

00:05:11.133 --> 00:05:13.234
Okay, whatever."
[ Laughter ]

00:05:13.234 --> 00:05:14.434
-And so you guys bonded?

00:05:14.434 --> 00:05:15.501
-She was really nice.
We bonded.

00:05:15.501 --> 00:05:16.701
I mean, I'm a big fan of hers.

00:05:16.701 --> 00:05:18.801
I used to go see her perform
at the Palace.

00:05:18.801 --> 00:05:20.901
Like, she's Courtney Love.
-She's a rock legend.

00:05:20.901 --> 00:05:22.434
Come on. Yeah.
-Yeah, I loved her.

00:05:22.434 --> 00:05:24.734
And also, she was fun
to play because she's --

00:05:24.734 --> 00:05:28.200
I think she's the total
opposite of me in real life.

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:30.267
I think she doesn't care
about breaking the rules

00:05:30.267 --> 00:05:32.834
and being a bad girl,
and I'm more like a good girl.

00:05:32.834 --> 00:05:33.968
So she was fun to play.

00:05:33.968 --> 00:05:35.834
-Yeah, exactly.
-I'm a good girl.

00:05:35.834 --> 00:05:38.067
-Yeah, of course you are.
-Not really.

00:05:40.601 --> 00:05:42.968
-Can you tell the story
about you and Will Ferrell?

00:05:42.968 --> 00:05:44.868
Because when you did
"Night at the Roxbury,"

00:05:44.868 --> 00:05:46.467
you had to do media training.

00:05:46.467 --> 00:05:49.100
-Yes, well, media training
is such a funny thing.

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:51.067
So when you first
get into show biz,

00:05:51.067 --> 00:05:52.200
you have to do
all these interviews

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:54.634
to sell the movie
for the studio

00:05:54.634 --> 00:05:56.133
to really get people
to go see it.

00:05:56.133 --> 00:05:58.367
So they thought that
Will Ferrell and Chris Kattan

00:05:58.367 --> 00:05:59.434
and I didn't know
anything about that.

00:05:59.434 --> 00:06:00.968
So they wanted to put us
through training

00:06:00.968 --> 00:06:02.968
and they take us
in a hotel room for 12 hours.

00:06:02.968 --> 00:06:04.567
And this woman with like
a scarf was like,

00:06:04.567 --> 00:06:06.901
"Okay, I'm going to
videotape you

00:06:06.901 --> 00:06:09.701
and I'm going to ask you why,
you know --

00:06:09.701 --> 00:06:11.501
Let's start with Will."
So she's videotaping.

00:06:11.501 --> 00:06:13.267
She was like,
"Why should I -- We'll start."

00:06:13.267 --> 00:06:14.734
It was like
a practice question.

00:06:14.734 --> 00:06:16.868
"Why should I go see
'A Night at the Roxbury'?"

00:06:16.868 --> 00:06:18.467
And Will was like,
"I don't know,

00:06:18.467 --> 00:06:20.834
maybe because
you'll win a million dollars."

00:06:20.834 --> 00:06:22.901
And she was like,
"Stop the camera." She was like,

00:06:22.901 --> 00:06:25.667
"That's not funny.
I don't think that's funny.

00:06:25.667 --> 00:06:27.434
Do you think that's funny?"
We were like, "Yeah.

00:06:27.434 --> 00:06:30.234
It's kind of funny."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:30.234 --> 00:06:31.667
-She kept yelling at you guys?

00:06:31.667 --> 00:06:33.767
-Yeah, no, yeah,
freezing the camera.

00:06:33.767 --> 00:06:36.300
We were in hysterics.
-I think I met with her.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:38.033
-You did?
-Yes, because everyone

00:06:38.033 --> 00:06:39.734
through "SNL" had to meet
this media trainer

00:06:39.734 --> 00:06:42.200
to teach you how to do press
and do interviews.

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:43.634
-Yes. Did you learn anything?

00:06:43.634 --> 00:06:45.534
-No! At all. No.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:45.534 --> 00:06:47.367
She was mean.
-Exactly.

00:06:47.367 --> 00:06:48.701
-It ruined my confidence.

00:06:48.701 --> 00:06:50.834
-That's so funny. Yeah.

00:06:50.834 --> 00:06:52.467
-I love -- By the way,
"Hello, Molly!"

00:06:52.467 --> 00:06:53.934
is now in paperback, buddy.

00:06:53.934 --> 00:06:57.133
That is a massive deal.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:57.133 --> 00:07:00.400
-"New York Times" best seller!

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:02.000
-Come on.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:04.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:04.534 --> 00:07:06.167
-Thank you.
-Amazing. Can you believe it?

00:07:06.167 --> 00:07:07.300
I love the stories.

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:10.567
It's so, so funny hearing
of your career.

00:07:10.567 --> 00:07:12.300
There's some heartbreaking
stories in here.

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:13.901
There's heartwarming stories.

00:07:13.901 --> 00:07:16.834
You talk about your dad, Jim,
who I love and I got to meet.

00:07:16.834 --> 00:07:21.000
-My dad loved you, Jimmy.
He loved you so much.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:24.033
He was like,
"That Jimmy Fallon. Oh!"

00:07:24.033 --> 00:07:26.834
He was like --
You were always so nice to him.

00:07:26.834 --> 00:07:28.300
Marci Klein's wedding,
You're like,

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:31.067
"Oh, my God, Mr. Shannon.
Mr. Shannon." He loved you.

00:07:31.067 --> 00:07:33.367
Jimmy makes
everyone feel so good.

00:07:33.367 --> 00:07:35.968
You're so charming
and wonderful.

00:07:35.968 --> 00:07:38.100
And my dad adored you.

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:39.501
-But here's what I read
in the book.

00:07:39.501 --> 00:07:42.234
That he kind of trained
you to act.

00:07:42.234 --> 00:07:43.634
-Yes.

00:07:43.634 --> 00:07:46.033
-He gave you, like, little
fun games to play with your --

00:07:46.033 --> 00:07:48.400
-Yeah, I call it the Jim
Shannon School of Acting.

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:50.300
It was when I was growing up.

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:53.534
My real taste and how I learned
how to act was from my father.

00:07:53.534 --> 00:07:55.968
So we would play this game
that he made up

00:07:55.968 --> 00:07:58.968
where we would have to answer
a fake telephone call

00:07:58.968 --> 00:08:00.968
and be very real.

00:08:00.968 --> 00:08:04.267
And so, for example, we used
to say "Shannon's residence"

00:08:04.267 --> 00:08:05.901
when we answer the phone.
So I would just go,

00:08:05.901 --> 00:08:08.133
"Hello, Shannon's residence.
This is Molly.

00:08:08.133 --> 00:08:10.767
Yeah."
And if it wasn't real,

00:08:10.767 --> 00:08:13.033
he would go "Stop!" you know,
and make you start over again.

00:08:13.033 --> 00:08:16.033
But we used to get so good --
we would get so good at it

00:08:16.033 --> 00:08:17.601
that say you were
my friend Jimmy over.

00:08:17.601 --> 00:08:19.934
And we were playing.
I could trick you.

00:08:19.934 --> 00:08:22.167
We would --
My dad would call the house

00:08:22.167 --> 00:08:24.868
to make it ring
so that I could trick my friend

00:08:24.868 --> 00:08:26.300
to thinking you
really had a coffee.

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:27.601
So I'd be like,
"Shannon's residence.

00:08:27.601 --> 00:08:31.400
This is Molly. Yeah.
Oh, yeah. Jimmy's right here.

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:33.100
Uh-huh.

00:08:33.100 --> 00:08:37.267
Yeah."
And then I would pull you in.

00:08:37.267 --> 00:08:40.067
-I want to play too.
-It's like that.

00:08:40.067 --> 00:08:41.434
-Alright, let me --
Alright, hold on.

00:08:41.434 --> 00:08:43.634
[ Laughter ]

00:08:43.634 --> 00:08:45.467
Fallon residence.
This is Jimmy.

00:08:47.701 --> 00:08:49.901
Oh, yeah.
Hold on. Just --

00:08:49.901 --> 00:08:52.434
Molly is on the show,
so I'm in the middle of this.

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:55.133
Lorne says hi.

00:08:55.133 --> 00:08:56.667
[ Laughter ]

00:08:56.667 --> 00:08:58.400
Yeah. Yeah. Okay, good. Yeah.

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:00.133
Ooh.

00:09:00.133 --> 00:09:03.667
Oh, that sounds --
that sounds dangerous.

00:09:03.667 --> 00:09:05.300
A Jet Ski?

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:06.901
-What?
-Okay.

00:09:06.901 --> 00:09:08.601
Alright. Sounds like fun.

00:09:08.601 --> 00:09:10.701
Alright, I'll give you
a ring later. Bye.

00:09:10.701 --> 00:09:15.234
[ Laughter, cheers, applause ]

00:09:15.234 --> 00:09:17.467
-I bailed a little bit
at the end.

00:09:17.467 --> 00:09:19.234
I bailed a little bit
at the end. Sorry, Jim.

00:09:19.234 --> 00:09:20.901
-I like how you say
"I'll give you a ring later."

00:09:20.901 --> 00:09:22.667
-Yeah, I made that up.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:22.667 --> 00:09:24.901
I'll give you a ring later.

00:09:24.901 --> 00:09:26.634
Let's talk about
"The Other Two," by the way.

00:09:26.634 --> 00:09:28.634
I saw the first episode
of season three.

00:09:28.634 --> 00:09:30.234
It's so funny.

00:09:30.234 --> 00:09:33.033
It is so funny and ridiculous.
Who are the writers?

00:09:33.033 --> 00:09:34.901
-Chris Kelly
and Sarah Schneider.

00:09:34.901 --> 00:09:36.267
They are so talented.

00:09:36.267 --> 00:09:38.300
This season cuts
three years ahead,

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:41.934
and it's really a season where
we take the biggest swings ever.

00:09:41.934 --> 00:09:43.534
It's going to be phenomenal.

00:09:43.534 --> 00:09:45.100
-Yeah, you you play Pat.

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:48.133
You are the mom
and you have a talk show

00:09:48.133 --> 00:09:49.501
and things are going
really well for you.

00:09:49.501 --> 00:09:51.601
And you have three kids?

00:09:51.601 --> 00:09:52.901
-Three kids. Yes.

00:09:52.901 --> 00:09:55.100
And my son is becoming
the next big pop superstar.

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:57.567
-So he becomes kind of like
a Justin Bieber type of thing.

00:09:57.567 --> 00:09:59.968
And the other two, which is what
the show is about, are going,

00:09:59.968 --> 00:10:01.601
"Hey, what about us?"
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:10:01.601 --> 00:10:03.400
-"We have lives, too.
We're somebody too."

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:05.334
-Exactly.
-And they are so funny.

00:10:05.334 --> 00:10:06.901
-Yes.
-It's brilliant.

00:10:06.901 --> 00:10:08.234
-Thank you so much.

00:10:08.234 --> 00:10:10.334
I feel so lucky
to be to be on the show.

00:10:10.334 --> 00:10:13.067
It's just -- I can't wait for
everybody to see season three.

00:10:13.067 --> 00:10:15.534
-They're going to see it here.
I want to show a clip.

00:10:15.534 --> 00:10:18.267
Here's Molly Shannon
in "The Other Two."

00:10:18.267 --> 00:10:19.400
Take a look at this.

00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:21.367
-We have a full home theater?

00:10:21.367 --> 00:10:23.801
-Oh, and are you guys going to
want snacks for the premiere?

00:10:23.801 --> 00:10:26.501
What do you kids like?
-You know I need Reese's Pieces.

00:10:26.501 --> 00:10:28.434
-Oh! Yes. Okay.

00:10:28.434 --> 00:10:30.934
When it comes to snacks,
there's nothing my family

00:10:30.934 --> 00:10:34.501
and I love more than
at Reese's Pieces.

00:10:34.501 --> 00:10:36.834
In five, four...

00:10:36.834 --> 00:10:38.067
[ Doorbell rings ]

00:10:38.067 --> 00:10:40.067
Ahhh!
-What is happening?

00:10:41.501 --> 00:10:43.801
-Hi, Pat.
Just dropping off some R.P. swag

00:10:43.801 --> 00:10:46.367
since we heard you're a fan.
-Thanks so much.

00:10:47.701 --> 00:10:49.801
Isn't this fun?

00:10:49.801 --> 00:10:52.734
Last week I tweeted
the upstairs toilet won't flush.

00:10:52.734 --> 00:10:54.100
-Why would you tweet that?

00:10:54.100 --> 00:10:56.100
-Well, that one was supposed
to be a text to Streeter,

00:10:56.100 --> 00:10:58.567
but now I have a lifetime supply
of Liquid-Plumr.

00:10:58.567 --> 00:11:01.667
It really is so convenient
to be powerful.

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:04.767
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:11:04.767 --> 00:11:06.167
-Molly Shannon, everybody!

00:11:06.167 --> 00:11:09.801
"The Other Two" premieres
May 4th on HBO Max.

00:11:09.801 --> 00:11:12.567
Catch her hosting
"Saturday Night Live"

